course: everyday
lesson: 253
title: Getting ready for school and the school day
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A school day goes well when you are ready: you have eaten breakfast, you have the right things for today's timetable, and you are on time when the bell rings.
explain: Many school rules exist so that adults always know where every child is: answering the register, asking to go to the toilet, and going back to the office if nobody comes to pick you up.
explain: Drills practise keeping safe: in a lockdown drill (practice for staying hidden inside when there may be danger outside the classroom) you stay silent and away from doors and windows; in an earthquake drill you drop, cover under a desk and hold on.
explain: If something is wrong, you tell an adult: a worry that makes you not want to go to school, or feeling ill in a lesson.
explain: A boy in Wellington hears the earthquake drill signal, drops under his desk, covers his head and holds on to the desk leg until his teacher says it is over.
fact: On PE day you wear or pack the sports clothes and trainers the school asks for and tie long hair back.
fact: If you do not want to go to school, you tell a parent honestly why, because a real problem can only be fixed if an adult knows.
fact: You eat at least something simple before school, because the brain needs food to concentrate.
fact: When the bell rings, you go straight to your line or classroom and get ready to listen.
fact: At the register (when the teacher calls out every name) you answer clearly, because the school must know which children are in the building in case of an emergency.
fact: You check the timetable the night before, so you bring the right things and know where to be.
fact: In corridors you walk on the correct side, do not run and keep your voice low.
fact: In a lockdown drill you stay silent, follow the teacher and keep away from doors and windows until told it is over.
fact: In an earthquake drill you drop, cover under a desk and hold on, because falling objects cause most injuries.
fact: If nobody comes to pick you up, you go back to the school office and tell a teacher; you never accept a lift you were not told about.
fact: Outside school you cross with the crossing guard or lollipop lady (a helper with a big round stop sign) and wait for their signal.
fact: If you feel ill in a lesson, you tell the teacher and ask to go to the nurse or office.
fact: To go to the toilet in a lesson, you ask politely and come back quickly.
fact: You follow the school rule on phones, usually keeping it switched off in your bag or handing it in.
exercise: It is PE day. What do you bring? | The sports clothes and trainers the school asks for | Your party shoes, because they are the shiniest | Nothing, because PE clothes are never really needed | Only a water bottle
exercise: You have long hair and it is PE. What should you do? | Tie it back | Leave it loose over your face | Cut it short at school | Hide it under a hat
exercise: One morning you do not want to go to school because of a quarrel. What do you do? | Tell a parent honestly why | Pretend to have a tummy ache | Hide in your room until it is too late to go | Say nothing and stay in bed
exercise: You are late and want to skip breakfast. What is better? | Eat something simple like toast or fruit | Eat nothing at all until lunch, so you are extra hungry | Drink only a fizzy drink | Eat a whole bag of sweets on the bus on the way to school
exercise: Why does breakfast matter before school? | The brain needs food to concentrate | It makes the school day go by shorter | Breakfast makes you grow taller at once | The teacher checks your plate every morning
exercise: The morning bell rings. What do you do? | Go to your line or classroom straight away | Finish your game first, then wander over | Go to the shop | Wait until the teacher has called you at least twice
exercise: The teacher calls your name for the register. What do you say? | "Here" or "Present", clearly | Nothing, because she can already see you | Your best friend's name, as a joke | "Later, please, I'm still busy"
exercise: Why does the school take the register? | To know which children are in the building in an emergency | To check who has the best and neatest handwriting in the class | To see who is tallest | To decide who gets the prizes at the end of term
exercise: When should you check your timetable? | The night before | Only on Friday afternoons | Never, the teacher will tell you | After school has started
exercise: How do you move along a busy corridor? | Walk on the correct side and keep your voice low | Run, so that you are not late for the next lesson | Push past the slow children | Shout to your friends at the other end
exercise: A lockdown drill begins. What do you do? | Stay silent and keep away from doors and windows | Run outside to the playground as fast as you can | Open the door to look | Call your parents loudly on your phone to tell them
exercise: An earthquake drill begins in your school in California. What do you do? | Drop, cover under a desk and hold on | Run out of the building at once | Stand by the window to watch outside | Stand in the very middle of the room
exercise: Nobody has come to pick you up after school. What do you do? | Go back to the office and tell a teacher | Walk home alone by the quickest way you know | Accept a lift from any parent you happen to see | Wait at the gate all by yourself until dark
exercise: How do you cross the road outside school? | With the crossing guard, waiting for their signal | Between the parked cars, where it is quickest | Wherever is fastest | Running across in a big group, so the drivers can see you
exercise: You feel sick in the middle of maths. What do you do? | Tell the teacher and ask to go to the nurse or office | Keep quiet until home time | Walk home on your own without telling anybody at all | Put your head down on the desk and say nothing to anyone
exercise: You need the toilet during a lesson. What do you do? | Ask the teacher politely, then come back quickly | Leave without telling anyone | Stay out in the corridor for the rest of the lesson | Wait until lunch, whatever happens
exercise: Your school says phones must stay off. Where is your phone in class? | Switched off in your bag or handed in | On your desk, so you can check messages | In your hand under the table where nobody can see | On loud in your pocket so you hear it ring
test: A girl in New York pretends to be ill because a classmate is bullying her. Why is telling the truth better? | Her parents can only fix the real problem if they know | Pretending to be ill is the quickest way to make the bully stop | Being ill is a good excuse that works forever | The teacher will never find out, so she can keep pretending all term
test: A boy in Canberra forgot to check his timetable and has no swimming kit on swimming day. What habit would help? | Checking the timetable the night before | Asking the teacher to cancel swimming for the whole class | Bringing every kit every day | Never doing swimming again
test: A fire breaks out and the teacher counts the children outside. Why is the morning register so important? | It shows exactly who is in the building | It shows who arrived first | It shows who did their homework | It tells the cook how many lunches to make
test: During a lockdown drill, a boy hears a knock and wants to open the classroom door. What is right? | Stay away from the door and stay silent | Open it to see who it is | Shout "Come in" loudly so they can hear | Go out to check the corridor yourself
test: In an earthquake, why is it safer under a sturdy desk than standing by a bookcase? | Falling objects cause most injuries, and the desk protects your head | Desks are heavy enough to stop the ground underneath them from shaking | Bookcases are always fixed down firmly to the wall | The desk is closer to the door
test: After school, a neighbour you hardly know offers a girl a lift home, but her mum did not say so. What should she do? | Say no and go back to the school office | Get in because she knows the neighbour's face | Walk home alone | Ask her friend to come too
test: A boy feels hungry and grumpy by 10 o'clock every morning. What might help? | Eating something before school | Sleeping in class | Eating nothing all day | Drinking a fizzy drink at break
test: Two children race each other down a crowded corridor. What can happen? | They can crash into others and fall | They will reach class early with no risk | The teacher will give them a medal | Nothing, corridors are wide
test: A boy needs the toilet and goes without asking. Why does the teacher mind? | The teacher needs to know where every child is | The toilet may be closed | It is rude to need the toilet during a lesson | He might miss a funny joke the teacher tells while he is out
test: mum hasn't come, a man i don't know says he'll drive me home | Go back to the office and tell a teacher | Go with him, he's being kind | Walk home alone, it's not far | Wait outside the gate alone till she comes
test: lockdown drill, can i peek out the door to see whats going on | No, stay silent, away from doors and windows | Yes, a quick look is fine | Yes, if the teacher isn't looking at you | Open the door a crack to listen
test: no breakfast today, i'm not hungry anyway | Eat something simple, your brain needs it | Skip it, lunch is enough to keep u going | Only eat sweets at break, that's energy | Drink nothing either, then u won't need the loo
test: i feel sick in maths. just stay put? | Tell the teacher and ask to go to the nurse | Keep quiet till home time, it'll probably pass | Walk out of the room without asking anyone first | Put your head down on the desk n have a sleep
explain: A boy in New Jersey called Ben skipped breakfast every morning; one day in PE he went dizzy, fainted and cut his chin on the gym floor.
explain: A girl in Scotland left late and dashed across the road to reach her bus; a car braked hard and missed her by a metre, and the driver was shaking.
fact: Children who skip breakfast find it harder to concentrate and can feel faint by mid-morning.
fact: Rushing because you are late is when many children run into roads without looking, so missing a bus is better than being hit.
test: bus is pulling up across the road, gonna leg it over | Stop and look both ways; better to miss the bus | Run, the driver always waits for kids who are running | Run, cars always stop for school kids near buses | Run but wave ur arms so all the cars can see you coming
test: Why did Ben go dizzy and faint in PE? | He had eaten nothing since the night before | PE is always far too hard for children | His trainers were too tight | He drank too much water at break
test: no time for brekkie, i'll just eat at lunch | Grab something quick, your body needs fuel to think | Fine, kids don't need breakfast, only grown-ups do | Fine, have sweets on the walk | Have a can of energy drink instead, that's even better
test: did u hear a kid was late, ran for the bus and ran right into the road and got clipped by a car? real lesson? | Missing the bus is better than being hit; don't rush the road | Run faster so you make the bus | Cars always stop for school kids, so the driver was to blame | Only cross when the bus is waiting
test: apparently a man said "your mum sent me" and tried to take a kid from outside school. what's the true lesson? | Never take a lift you weren't told about; go to the office | Go with him if he knows your mum's name and where you live | Go if he has a nice car | Wait outside alone until he gets bored and leaves
test: my mate says if u skip breakfast u faint every single time. probably exaggerated? | Every time is stretched, but skipping makes focus hard | Yes, everyone faints without breakfast | No, breakfast never matters | Yes, but only on Mondays
exam: Identify the reason for which pupils answer the register clearly. | The school must know which children are present in case of emergency | It is a test of loud speaking | The register determines the marks awarded at the end of each term | It is a custom without purpose
exam: Which of the following constitutes appropriate conduct during an earthquake drill? | Drop, take cover under a desk and hold on | Run out of the building at once | Stand beside a window in order to observe | Hide in a corridor beside the tall shelves
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice where nobody arrives to collect a pupil. | He returns to the school office and informs a teacher | He accepts a lift from any adult known by sight | He walks home alone by a new route | He waits outside the gate until dark
exam: Ascertain the appropriate conduct during a lockdown drill. | One remains silent, follows the teacher and keeps from doors and windows | One goes to the playground at once | One telephones one's parents loudly | One looks out of the window to see
